            r2_name: `Monday 8 March 2027 · Week 2: the workflows`,
            r3_name: `Monday 15 March 2027 · Week 3: automating`,
            r4_name: `Monday 22 March 2027 · Week 4: data and the archive`,
            round_done: `Four weeks closed`,
            r1_brief: `Monday 1 March 2027. Nerea Hernández Calvo, an administrative technician, started in February in the office of Instalaciones Navaleda, an air-conditioning and electrical installation company in Salamanca with 26 people, and Julián Sánchez, the manager, has asked her to "bring the office up to date". Every week about 110 supplier invoices come in (40% still on paper), about 90 delivery notes from the technicians, 12 budgets that must be signed and about 3 electronic notifications from the tax agency, Social Security or other administrations. Nerea and Rosa, the veteran administrator, have about 52 hours a week for paperwork; each hour costs EUR 16 and each overtime hour EUR 8 more. Today you decide how invoices are recorded, where documents come in and who watches the notifications.`,
            r2_brief: `Monday 8 March. The first week is in the data. Julián signs the printed invoices on Fridays and some are paid late; a supplier invoice averages EUR 780 and 30% of suppliers offer a 1.5% prompt-payment discount. Budgets are printed, the customer signs them at the office or by post and they are scanned; a signed job leaves about EUR 450 of margin. Everyone keeps documents in their own folders and the paper in boxes. Today you decide how invoices are approved, how customers sign and where everything is filed.`,
            r3_brief: `Monday 15 March. Customers owe about EUR 52,000 in overdue invoices and Rosa calls them when she can, about 3 hours a week. Julián wants a dashboard with charts. Rosa types every supplier transfer into the bank’s website. The technicians still hand in paper delivery notes and 3% are lost without being invoiced; each delivery note is about EUR 140 of work. The new delivery-note app is already installed on their phones. Today you decide what is automated first, how suppliers are paid and how the change reaches the technicians.`,
            r4_brief: `Monday 22 March. Three matters nobody wanted to look at. On Friday 26 February two requests arrived: a former technician asks for a copy of his personal data and a customer asks for hers to be erased; the one-month deadline expires on Friday 26 March. The 140 CVs from the autumn job offer are still in the mailbox. And 9 boxes of papers from 2014 to 2026 fill the storeroom, so the company pays EUR 30 a week for an outside storage unit. The accountants remind everyone that the books and their supporting documents are kept for six years. Today you decide what is done with the requests, the CVs and the boxes.`,
            brief_done: `The four weeks are closed. Review the final summary and the history table.`,
            r1_heading: `Week 1 · What comes in`,
            r1_intro: `They apply for all four weeks. The set-up is done this week.`,
            r2_heading: `Week 2 · The workflows`,
            r2_intro: `They apply from week 2 to week 4. The first week ran as always: approval on paper on Fridays, budgets signed on paper and everyone with their own folders.`,
            r3_heading: `Week 3 · Automating`,
            r3_intro: `They apply to weeks 3 and 4.`,
            r4_heading: `Week 4 · Data and the archive`,
            r4_intro: `They apply to week 4.`,
